<h2 id="phone">What stays on the phone</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Your position</b>, read from GPS for the map, the guidance and the alerts. It is sent nowhere. Outside an outing, the app only asks for an approximate position, when you ask for the nearest areas.</li>
<li><b>Your tracks and outings</b>, recorded on the phone. Android backup is turned off for the app: they are not copied to the cloud.</li>
<li><b>Your day plans.</b> The app writes your plan as a text message for the person you choose; it leaves the phone only if you send it yourself.</li>
<li><b>The voice</b>: announcements are clips recorded in advance in the pack and assembled on the phone. What has no clip is read by the phone's own voice.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="server">What the server receives</h2>

<h3>Pack downloads and updates</h3>
<p>The app downloads the pack of the ski area you choose, the way a browser downloads a file, then updates the installed packs once a day if you leave automatic updates on. The server sees the IP address of the connection and the file requested, and so the area you chose. It does not receive your position. There is no account.</p>
<p>How long the server keeps its logs will be set before the release and written here.</p>

<p>The app sends nothing else to the server.</p>

<h2 id="never">What we do not do</h2>
<ul>
<li>No advertising.</li>
<li>No audience measurement and no advertising tracker in the app.</li>
<li>No sale of data. Nothing goes to a third party.</li>
<li>No public map of your off-piste tracks.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="permissions">Android permissions</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Precise location</b>, including with the screen off during an outing you started, for voice guidance. A notification stays visible while guidance runs. <b>Approximate location</b>, once, when you ask for the nearest areas.</li>
<li><b>Notifications</b>, for guidance, the reminder before the time to be back and the alerts.</li>
<li><b>Vibration</b>, for the vibration codes of the guidance.</li>
<li><b>Internet access</b>, to download and update the packs.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Google Play</h2>
<p>The purchase goes through <a href={link('googlePlay', 'en')} rel="noopener">Google Play</a>. Google handles the payment under its own privacy rules; Traces Blanches does not receive your payment details.</p>

<h2 id="site">This website</h2>
<p>Static pages served by <a href={link('githubPages', 'en')} rel="noopener">GitHub Pages</a>: no cookies, no analytics. A script of a few lines handles the language. On your first visit to the home page, it reads your browser's preferred language and opens the French version if that is French. When you pick FR or EN at the top of a page, it keeps that choice in your browser's local storage. A second script of a few lines opens and closes the menu on a phone and runs the pause button of the home page pictures; it stores nothing. Nothing is sent.</p>
<p>GitHub keeps its own server logs, see its <a rel="noopener" href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">privacy statement</a>.</p>

<h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
<p>For a question about your data, <a rel="noopener" href="https://github.com/tracesblanches/tracesblanches/issues">ask it on GitHub</a>. For a security problem, follow the <a rel="noopener" href="https://github.com/tracesblanches/.github/blob/main/SECURITY.md">security policy</a>.</p>
